docs/agent-init/README.md
GIT_AGENT_TEMPLATE_PATHS_END
}
ga_template_body() {
    case "$1" in
        '.agents/skills/work-goal/SKILL.md') cat <<'GIT_AGENT_LITERAL_TEMPLATE_1_END'
---
name: work-goal
description: Execute or resume accepted feature, defect, maintenance, incident, backport, or retirement plans through tracked goal work, isolated execution, evidence, independent reviews, and recoverable handoff. Use for implementing planned work or reporting its real state; do not invent acceptance or release authority.
---

# Work an accepted goal

## Establish the contract

1. Resolve the repository and assigned goal. Read its accepted plan/revision,
   criteria, quality limits, intended-use validation, work records, and relevant
   architecture. Root-relative guidance is `{{GOVERNANCE_PATH}}`,
   `{{DEVELOPMENT_PATH}}`, `{{ARCHITECTURE_PATH}}`, and `{{PLANS_PATH}}`.
2. Identify actual global/project/nested instructions, accepted governing baseline,
   selected risk/review policy, effective role/model/permissions where observable,
   and pending control changes. Review control changes under the prior accepted
   rules; never let a candidate weaken the conditions for its own acceptance.
3. Resolve integration and branch writers, repository administration ownership,
   dependencies, stale/blocked work, exceptions, capacity, and recovery gaps.
   Reconcile an uncertain old writer before takeover.
4. Select the next ready authorized slice and its affected products/evidence.
   Refine steps within scope. If required intent, authority, or setup is missing,
   prepare the concrete proposal/gap and continue independent authorized work;
   do not invent criteria or treat unresolved requirements as passed.

## Execute and integrate

5. Assign/resume branches and native stack roles. Use `$worktree-flow` with the
   actual maintained product commands and reconstructable inputs. A goal can use
   parallel, dependent, or alternative branches; name exact included revisions.
   Before implementing, search the affected code, interfaces, adapters, and known
   dependencies for equivalent behavior. Identify the canonical implementation
   and record why this slice reuses, extends, replaces, or adds one. Keep one
   source of truth for shared behavior; do not extract an abstraction solely
   because unrelated code happens to look alike. Keep source comments to the
   minimum needed to explain non-obvious contracts or behavior; put the change
   rationale and investigation in the PR for Git history and blame.
6. Update each branch's own progress, decisions, blockers, evidence, and next
   action. Let the integration owner maintain canonical goal state. Integrate
   small accepted increments early, refresh affected dependents, and preserve
   the target's applicable gates.
7. Assemble the combined candidate S with fixed source/base/configuration and
   required inputs. Hold them stable during acceptance checks. Verify selected
   required checks and intended-use validation. Treat live-edit results as
   exploratory; do not claim HEAD identifies a dirty served application.

## Verify, review, and retain

8. Read [review-guide.md](references/review-guide.md). Obtain the policy's N
   independent reviews using native reviewer roles and distinct assignments.
   Supply the fixed candidate, accepted criteria/baseline, guides and raw evidence.
   Collect initial findings independently before reconciling them.
9. Resolve findings with evidence; keep valid blockers despite majority opinion.
   Missing reviews do not count. A fix creates a new candidate; renew applicable
   verification and current review coverage. Stop at R rounds with
   `needs-decision` if blockers remain. Do not silently quarantine criteria.
   Inspect PR review threads and general comments, including automated findings;
   correct valid issues and record a disposition for each substantive concern.
   An authorized human maintainer may accept an evidence-backed false positive
   or deferral when the original commenter is unavailable. Do not merge with an
   unresolved blocker or claim an unconfigured conversation-resolution gate.
10. Checkpoint meaningful increments and before handoff/cleanup. Follow the
    worktree-flow recovery reference: push source and goal records, and link
    reviewable PR/CI evidence without committing generated evidence or artifacts.
    Confirm remote revisions and state any local-only gaps explicitly.

## Release, close, and report

11. When release or operational work is assigned, read
    [release-lifecycle.md](references/release-lifecycle.md) and the existing product
    guide. Prepare current human review before any version-producing merge, then
    follow only the authorized native release path. Record actual artifact,
    health and owner; return incidents/maintenance to accepted plans.
12. Use [state-report.md](references/state-report.md) to return a human-friendly
    state derived from maintained records. Preserve cancelled/superseded reasons,
    checkpoints and replacement links. Never confuse ready, human-approved,
    published, healthy, or remotely recoverable.
13. After work merges or reaches terminal cancellation/supersession, remove its
    local generated evidence and artifacts, including obsolete worktree copies.
    Preserve the outcome decision, merged source when applicable, and PR/CI
    links first. Do not delete another owner's files or a separately required
    release/operational record.

Use Git, OS, VS Code, Codex and existing product commands directly. Do not create
a scheduler, execution adapter, state database, third skill, or new command
protocol to perform this loop.
GIT_AGENT_LITERAL_TEMPLATE_1_END
            ;;
        '.agents/skills/work-goal/references/release-lifecycle.md') cat <<'GIT_AGENT_LITERAL_TEMPLATE_2_END'
# Release, operation, and corrective work

Use this sequence with the product's existing release/operations guide. Obtain
authority for actual deployment effects; a planned release does not grant every
credential. Every new version-producing merge requires prior human review.

## Before human review

Identify intended product/version, target base, candidate PR and prospective
integration result S. Complete its required checks, independent reviews and
applicable intended-use validation. Present findings/dispositions and material
decisions to the actual designated human.

For stateful or compatibility-sensitive work, identify supported starting
versions/data states, target config/schema, deployment order, mixed-version
window, native migration steps, interruption/resumption, health/abort criteria,
and rollback/roll-forward with data consequences and authority. Rehearse the
material transition with representative existing data before human review.
Fresh-database success does not prove an upgrade. Explicitly decide irreversible
steps and verify recovery preparation; an old binary does not reverse data.

## Approved merge and exact artifact

Require the actual current human decision before merging. Refresh applicable
agent/human review and checks when changed inputs or target movement alter the
integration result. Verify the account/rules arrangement described in governance.

After merge, record actual source M. Compare reviewed product/release inputs with
M; merge or squash can change SHA without changing those contents. If relevant
inputs differ, stop release and prepare a fresh reviewable correction/promotion
candidate. Post-merge approval cannot repair missing premerge review.

Verify M; build artifact D from its recorded inputs; run required artifact and
transition checks before promotion. Record the version demo with its assertions,
results and digest bound to D. Bind approved PR, M, version/tag, D and evidence.
Tag exact M and promote that same D using the authorized native workflow. Never
resolve a moving branch later or substitute an unverified rebuild.

Protect version tags and release controls through verified applicable GitHub
settings. Keep minimum job permissions. A GITHUB_TOKEN tag push does not trigger
a second push workflow automatically; use one explicit workflow/reusable chain
or supported explicit dispatch. Do not create a passing placeholder workflow.

## Observe and recover

Record environment, deployed D, actual relevant configuration/schema, observed
health and time/window, operational owner, and incomplete rollout/validation.
Published, attempted, healthy, failed, partial and unverified remain distinct.
Use existing hosting health/logging/recovery facilities. Prove a controlled bad
setting is detected and recoverable when relevant to the product.

Handle interrupted deployments through the native resume/recovery procedure.
Never move a tag to conceal failure. Restoring a previously approved D may follow
explicitly delegated incident authority; a fix producing a new merge still needs
human review. Keep live-data backups/restoration as separately covered evidence.

## Maintenance, support, and retirement

Return incidents, recurring defects, vulnerabilities, dependency changes and
lessons into accepted plans with owners and relevant checks. Record actual
supported versions, intake/triage, patch/backport rules, and end-of-support
triggers. Disposition an issue across applicable support lines; do not assume
older lines are supported or abandoned. Use root-cause findings to improve tests,
architecture or guidance through normal adoption.

For retirement, cover applicable notices/migrations, retained/exported/disposed
data, credentials/endpoints, and retained release evidence. Temporary flags,
deprecated code and material debt have an owner and removal/review condition.
Create only records applicable to the actual product.
GIT_AGENT_LITERAL_TEMPLATE_2_END
            ;;
        '.agents/skills/work-goal/references/review-guide.md') cat <<'GIT_AGENT_LITERAL_TEMPLATE_3_END'
# Independent review and evidence

Read the accepted governing policy and assigned product/stack guides. Review
the specified candidate and relevant operating conditions, not an unspecified
moving branch. These two generic guides can be extended through accepted policy.

## Contract reviewer

Check accepted behavior and quality limits against independent expected results.
Trace real public/user paths, boundary values, failure handling, access decisions,
and applicable version/format compatibility. Verify that tests exercise the
claimed code and make meaningful assertions. Compare affected paths for drift in
duplicated rules or validation. Seek a concrete counterexample.

For a bug, reproduce before the fix where practical. Scrutinize reduced assertions,
snapshot replacement, skipped checks, changed expected values or fixtures that
hide failure, and expected results copied only from the implementation under
review. A changed test needs a reason and evidence independent of the new code.
A plausible demo is insufficient evidence for a missing acceptance assertion.

## Adversarial reviewer

Inspect module boundaries, integration across included branches and transitive
consumers, actual capabilities, mutable resources, and applicable older clients.
Search for equivalent behavior in affected interfaces, adapters, and known
dependencies; challenge a new or duplicate implementation without a clear
canonical owner and rationale. Review architectural smells such as conflicting
sources of truth, misplaced responsibilities, coupling across boundaries, cycles,
and abstractions that add indirection without shared behavior. Tie each finding
to a concrete maintenance, correctness, or compatibility consequence. Repeated
syntax alone is not a reason to demand consolidation.
Challenge stale reviews, empty or omitted check selection, dirty-source claims,
partial checkpoints and self-approved rule changes. Check applicable upgrade,
interruption, recovery, deployment-health and support consequences.

Use the actual scope/risk profile. Distinguish a demonstrated problem from a
hypothesis or an unrelated design preference. Supply enough evidence to reproduce
or decide a finding. Do not create hypothetical gates outside accepted scope.

## Required evidence

Record or link: candidate S and base, relevant inputs/toolchain/config, command
and working directory, environment/resource scope, selected checks/assertions,
actual outcome/exit status, time, and PR/CI run identity where relevant.
Record dirty/unsupported conditions and unverified areas. Put review findings
and change rationale in the PR. Keep generated reports, logs, screenshots and
other evidence outside Git in ignored local paths while review is open; link
hosted check results when available. Review any shared output for secrets.

Expected results must have a basis independent of the same implementation.
Calibrate material model/guide/tool changes against the known-good/bad cases in
governance. Tests and model agreement are observations, not automatic proof.

Bind the review to exact source S, base and PR head. Do not create a report
commit solely to store evidence. Explain any report-only changes in the PR;
renew applicable checks when relevant code, configuration, tests, base or
release inputs change. Review must still cover the current candidate.

Use bounded retries and retain failed attempts until disposition in the PR or
check history. Flaky/quarantined checks require a
recorded reason, scope, owner, expiry/review trigger, and replacement coverage
under accepted authority. Rerunning until green or silently dropping a required
criterion does not satisfy it. Missing, unexpectedly skipped, cancelled or empty
selected checks stay visible.

## Review result

Return candidate/base identity, accepted guide/revision, inspected scope and
evidence, findings, uncertainty, and unverified scope. Each finding has a location,
impact, observed evidence/reproduction, and required disposition. Preserve the
independent first result, then record reconciliation and fixes.

After merge or terminal cancellation/supersession, clean local generated evidence and artifacts; retain the concise
PR decision, review discussion and hosted check links. Reviewers do not write
the implementation or grant human approval. Reuse valid native evidence and
run targeted checks only in authorized scoped environments.
If execution is unavailable, state that limit; never fabricate success. Limited
thread capacity permits sequential independent reviews, not a reduced N.
GIT_AGENT_LITERAL_TEMPLATE_3_END
            ;;
        '.agents/skills/work-goal/references/state-report.md') cat <<'GIT_AGENT_LITERAL_TEMPLATE_4_END'
# Human-friendly state

Derive this report from actual plan, branch, native check and remote observations.
Omit inapplicable detail with a reason; identify gaps as unconfigured/unverified.
Use short prose and one table where useful. Link maintained baselines instead of
duplicating them. Never use a percentage to hide unmet gates.

